messages:   18%|#8        | 109/600 [09:47<20:55,  2.56s/it][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is rough. Dave is blue. Dave is cold. Dave is red. Dave is rough. Dave is round. Gary is round. Harry is cold. Harry is rough. All round things are blue. If something is cold then it is blue. Blue, red things are round. If something is rough then it is red. All big, red things are cold. All round things are green. Round, cold things are big. If something is green then it is blue.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Dave is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\nOkay, so I have this logic problem to solve, and I need to figure out whether the statement "Dave is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given information to make it easier to process.The context is a set of statements, some of which are conditional or relate different attributes.Let me go through each one and see how they connect.\n\n1.Anne is big.\n2.Anne is rough.\n3.Dave is blue.\n4.Dave is cold.\n5.Dave is red.\n6.Dave is rough.\n7.Dave is round.\n8.Gary is round.\n9.Harry is cold.\n10.Harry is rough.\n11.All round things are blue.\n12.If something is cold then it is blue.\n13.Blue, red things are round.\n14.If something is rough then it is red.\n15.All big, red things are cold.\n16.All round things are green.\n17.Round, cold things are big.\n18.If something is green then it is blue.\n\nAlright, that\'s a lot.Now, the question is about Dave being big.So I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly tells us whether Dave is big or not.\n\nLet me start by looking at what we know about Dave.From points 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7, Dave is blue, cold, red, rough, and round.So Dave has several attributes: blue, cold, red, rough, and round.\n\nNow, let\'s see what we can infer from these attributes.From point 12, if something is cold, then it is blue.But we already know Dave is blue and cold, so that checks out.Also, from point 13, blue, red things are round.Since Dave is both blue and red, he must be round, which we already know from point 7.\n\nPoint 14 says if something is rough, then it is red.Dave is rough and red, so that\'s consistent.\n\nPoint 11 says all round things are blue.Since Dave is round and blue, that\'s fine.\n\nPoint 16 says all round things are green.Wait, that\'s conflicting because point 7 says Dave is round, and point 11 says all round things are blue.But point 16 says all round things are green.Hmm, that seems contradictory.So maybe there\'s an error in the context, but I\'ll proceed as given.Dave is not big because he is round and cold, which contradicts the statement.</think>.\n\nThe statement "Dave is big" is **True** because Dave is round and cold, and according to the context, round, cold things are big. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
