messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe bald eagle eats the cow. The bald eagle is blue. The cat visits the bald eagle. The cat visits the rabbit. The cow is blue. The cow sees the rabbit. The rabbit eats the bald eagle. The rabbit is cold. The rabbit sees the cow. The rabbit visits the cat. If something visits the cow and it sees the cow then it visits the bald eagle. If something is nice then it is big. If something visits the bald eagle then it is nice. If something is cold and it visits the cow then the cow sees the cat. If something is round and it eats the bald eagle then it sees the bald eagle. If something visits the cat then it visits the rabbit. If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow then it visits the cow.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The rabbit is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve.Let me try to break it down step by step.The question is asking whether the statement "The rabbit is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.The options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown.\n\nFirst, I\'ll read through the context carefully to understand all the given information.The context is a series of statements connected by "if" and "then" clauses, which I think are conditional statements.So, I need to parse each of these statements to see what they imply.\n\nLet me list out all the given statements:\n\n1.The bald eagle eats the cow.\n2.The bald eagle is blue.\n3.The cat visits the bald eagle.\n4.The cat visits the rabbit.\n5.The cow is blue.\n6.The cow sees the rabbit.\n7.The rabbit eats the bald eagle.\n8.The rabbit is cold.\n9.The rabbit sees the cow.\n10.The rabbit visits the cat.\n11.If something visits the cow and it sees the cow, then it visits the bald eagle.\n12.If something is nice, then it is big.\n13.If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice.\n14.If something is cold and it visits the cow, then the cow sees the cat.\n15.If something is round and it eats the bald eagle, then it sees the bald eagle.\n16.If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit.\n17.If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow, then it visits the cow.\n\nWow, that\'s a lot.I need to figure out the relationships between these entities: bald eagle, cow, cat, rabbit, and their interactions.\n\nLet me start by mapping out who does what and who interacts with whom.\n\nFrom statement 1: Bald eagle eats the cow.So, the bald eagle is a predator of the cow.\n\nFrom statement 2: Bald eagle is blue.So, the bald eagle\'s color is blue.\n\nStatement 3: Cat visits the bald eagle.So, the cat goes to the bald eagle.\n\nStatement 4: Cat visits the rabbit.So, the cat also goes to the rabbit.\n\nStatement 5: Cow is blue.So, the cow\'s color is blue.\n\nStatement 6: Cow sees the rabbit.So, the cow observes the rabbit.\n\nStatement 7: Rabbit eats the bald eagle.Wait, that\'s interesting because earlier the bald eagle eats the cow, but here the rabbit eats the bald eagle.So, the rabbit is a predator of the bald eagle.\n\nStatement 8: Rabbit is cold.So, the rabbit has the temperature property of being cold.\n\nStatement 9: Rabbit sees the cow.So, the rabbit observes the cow.\n\nStatement 10: Rabbit visits the cat.So, the rabbit goes to the cat.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the conditional statements.\n\nStatement 11: If something visits the cow and it sees the cow, then it visits the bald eagle.So, visiting the cow and seeing the cow leads to visiting the bald eagle.\n\nStatement 12: If something is nice, then it is big.So, niceness implies size.\n\nStatement 13: If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice.So, visiting the bald eagle implies niceness.\n\nStatement 14: If something is cold and it visits the cow, then the cow sees the cat.So, being cold and visiting the cow causes the cow to see the cat.\n\nStatement 15: If something is round and it eats the bald eagle, then it sees the bald eagle.So, being round and eating the bald eagle leads to seeing the bald eagle.\n\nStatement 16: If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit.So, visiting the cat leads to visiting the rabbit.\n\nStatement 17: If something visits the rabbit and it sees the cow, then it visits the cow.So, visiting the rabbit and seeing the cow leads to visiting the cow.\n\nNow, I need to see if any of these conditionals can help me determine whether the rabbit is big or not.\n\nLet me start by trying to find any direct information about the rabbit\'s size.\n\nLooking through the statements, I don\'t see any explicit statement about the rabbit\'s size.So, maybe the answer is unknown.But let me check if any of the conditionals can imply it.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If something is nice, then it is big.So, niceness leads to being big.But I don\'t know if anything is nice or not.\n\nStatement 13: If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice.So, visiting the bald eagle makes something nice, which in turn makes it big (from statement 12).So, if something visits the bald eagle, it\'s nice and big.\n\nNow, who visits the bald eagle?From statement 3, the cat visits the bald eagle.So, the cat visits the bald eagle, which makes the cat nice and big (since visiting the bald eagle implies niceness, and niceness implies being big).\nThe rabbit is big based on the given information.</think>.\n\nThe statement "The rabbit is big" cannot be determined as true or false based on the given context. The context provides information about various entities and their interactions but does not explicitly state whether the rabbit is big. While the cat is known to be big, there is no direct information linking the rabbit\'s size. Therefore, the correct answer is:. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 23%|##2       | 136/600 [14:59<39:58,  5.17s/it]
