messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is cold. Anne is young. Charlie is cold. Erin is big. Fiona is blue. Fiona is red. Fiona is smart. If something is big and young then it is smart. If something is big and red then it is young. All cold things are red. If something is green then it is cold. If Anne is blue and Anne is smart then Anne is young. All smart, young things are green. If something is red and big then it is young. If something is big then it is young.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Erin is not green.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\nOkay, so I have this logic problem to solve, and I need to figure out whether the statement "Erin is not green" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given premises to make sure I don\'t miss anything.The context provided is a set of statements, each of which seems to relate different entities (like Anne, Charlie, Erin, Fiona) with various attributes (cold, young, big, blue, red, smart, green).My goal is to determine the color of Erin and whether the statement about her not being green is true or false.\n\nLet me list out all the premises:\n\n1.Anne is cold.\n2.Anne is young.\n3.Charlie is cold.\n4.Erin is big.\n5.Fiona is blue.\n6.Fiona is red.\n7.Fiona is smart.\n8.If something is big and young, then it is smart.\n9.If something is big and red, then it is young.\n10.All cold things are red.\n11.If something is green, then it is cold.\n12.If Anne is blue and Anne is smart, then Anne is young.\n13.All smart, young things are green.\n14.If something is red and big, then it is young.\n15.If something is big, then it is young.\n\nWow, that\'s a lot.Let me try to organize this information.\n\nFirst, let\'s note down the entities involved: Anne, Charlie, Erin, Fiona.The attributes are cold, young, big, blue, red, smart, green.\n\nStarting with Fiona, since she\'s mentioned multiple times.From premises 5, 6, and 7: Fiona is blue, red, and smart.So Fiona is blue, red, and smart.\n\nFrom premise 13: All smart, young things are green.So if something is smart and young, it\'s green.But Fiona is smart, but we don\'t know if she\'s young yet.Wait, premise 12 says if Anne is blue and smart, then she\'s young.But Fiona is blue and smart, but premise 12 is about Anne, not Fiona.So maybe that doesn\'t apply directly.\n\nWait, premise 12: If Anne is blue and Anne is smart, then Anne is young.So Anne being blue and smart leads to her being young.But Fiona is blue and smart, but we don\'t know if she\'s young.So maybe Fiona isn\'t necessarily young.\n\nBut let\'s see what else we can figure out about Fiona.She\'s red (premise 6) and big?Wait, no, premise 14 says if something is red and big, then it\'s young.But Fiona is red, but we don\'t know if she\'s big.Wait, premise 8: If something is big and young, then it\'s smart.But Fiona is big?Wait, no, premise 4 says Erin is big.So Fiona isn\'t necessarily big.\n\nWait, let me clarify: premise 4 says Erin is big.So Erin is big.Fiona is blue, red, and smart, but we don\'t know about her size.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the premises involving Erin.Erin is big (premise 4).So Erin is big.Now, from premise 9: If something is big and red, then it\'s young.So Erin is big, but we don\'t know if she\'s red.If she\'s big and red, she\'s young.But we don\'t know yet.\n\nFrom premise 15: If something is big, then it is young.So Erin is big, so according to premise 15, Erin must be young.So Erin is young.\n\nWait, so Erin is big and young.From premise 8: If something is big and young, then it is smart.So Erin is big and young, so she must be smart.Therefore, Erin is smart.\n\nSo Erin is big, young, and smart.\n\nNow, from premise 13: All smart, young things are green.So Erin is smart and young, so she must be green.Therefore, Erin is green.\n\nErin is not green, so the statement "Erin is not green" is true.</think>\n\nThe statement "Erin is not green" is false because Erin is green based on the given premises.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 26%|##5       | 153/600 [15:23<10:31,  1.41s/it]
