      "text": " A) True.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nFrom the context, statement 15: If someone visits the bald eagle and the bald eagle visits the squirrel, then the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\nWe know that the bald eagle visits the squirrel (statement 4). So, if someone visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\nBut who is the someone? The someone is the subject of the conditional. So, if X visits the bald eagle, then BE chases S.\n\nBut do we know that X visits BE? Let's see.\n\nFrom statement 18: If someone visits BE, they need BE.\n\nFrom statement 17: If someone chases the lion and visits BE, they need BE.\n\nFrom statement 19: If someone chases the lion, they visit BE.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone is red, they chase the lion.\n\nFrom statement 5: If someone is red, they chase the lion.\n\nSo, if someone is red, they chase the lion, who then visits BE.\n\nBut do we know if someone is red? Yes, the tiger is red (statement 11).\n\nSo, the tiger is red. From statement 16, if someone is red, they chase the lion. So, the tiger, being red, chases the lion.\n\nFrom statement 19, if someone chases the lion, they visit BE. So, the tiger chases the lion, so the tiger visits BE.\n\nFrom statement 15, if someone visits BE and BE visits S, then BE chases S.\n\nWe know that BE visits S (statement 4). So, if the tiger visits BE, then BE chases S.\n\nTherefore, the tiger visits BE, and BE visits S, so BE chases S.\n\nThus, the statement \"The bald eagle chases the squirrel\" is true.\n\nSo, the answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is true because the conditions lead to the conclusion that the bald eagle chases the squirrel.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
