ce (7) \u2192 Charlie is smart (14).\n\nCharlie is smart (14) \u2192 Charlie is blue (16).\n\nCharlie is blue (16) \u2192 Charlie is furry (17).\n\nCharlie is furry (17) \u2192 Charlie is cold (6) \u2192 Charlie is smart (18).\n\nWait, but Charlie is already smart from being nice, so this is consistent.\n\nBut wait, point 11: If someone is furry and nice, then they are cold. So, if someone is furry, they must be cold if they are nice. But Charlie is nice and cold, but not necessarily furry. So, that's okay because the converse isn't stated.\n\nSo, Charlie is nice \u2192 smart.\n\nCharlie is smart \u2192 blue.\n\nCharlie is blue \u2192 furry.\n\nCharlie is furry \u2192 cold.\n\nCharlie is cold \u2192 big.\n\nSo, Charlie is big, cold, nice, young, and smart.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Charlie is not smart\" is false.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought maybe there's a contradiction because Charlie isn't mentioned as blue, but point 16 says all smart people are blue, so if Charlie is smart, he must be blue. So, the context doesn't explicitly say Charlie is blue, but since he's smart, he must be. So, the context allows us to conclude that Charlie is blue, even if it's not explicitly stated.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Charlie is not smart\" is false because Charlie is smart.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Charlie is not smart\" is **False** because Charlie is nice, and according to the context, being nice implies being smart.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
