s cold (statement 9) and Gary is nice (statement 10).So Gary is both cold and nice.\n\nNext, I\'ll try to connect these attributes to other properties.Let\'s see what we can derive.\n\nFirst, since Gary is cold, from statement 17, "Cold things are big," we can infer that Gary is big.So Gary is big and cold.\n\nNow, since Gary is big and cold, from statement 16, "All cold, big things are red," we can conclude that Gary is red.So Gary is red.\n\nNow, Gary is red.Let\'s see what else we can say about red things.From statement 19, "If Fiona is cold, then Fiona is red," but that\'s about Fiona, not Gary.However, from statement 13, "All nice, red things are round," since Gary is nice and red, this statement tells us that Gary is round.So Gary is round.\nGary is not round, as he is not nice and red or rough and cold.</think>\n\nThe statement "Gary is round" is true based on the given context.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
