, young thing.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the statements that connect these attributes to other properties.\n\nStatement 12: Furry, green things are blue.So, if something is furry and green, it\'s blue.Gary is furry and green, so Gary is blue.That\'s a key point.\n\nStatement 17: If Gary is furry, then Gary is blue.Since Gary is furry, this statement directly tells us Gary is blue.So that\'s confirmed.\n\nNow, moving on, since Gary is blue, let\'s see what else we can deduce about him.\n\nStatement 14: Red, furry things are blue.Gary is not red, as blue and young things are red according to statement 20.Gary is blue and young, so he must be red based on statement 20.</think>.\n\nBased on the given context, Gary is determined to be red through the following logical steps:.\n\n1. Gary is furry and green, making him blue (statements 12 and 17).\n2. As a blue and young individual (statements 11 and 20), Gary is red.\n\nThus, the statement "Gary is red" is **True**. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
