      "text": " C) Unknown.\n\nWait, but in the initial analysis, I thought the bear likes the mouse because the rabbit is round and sees the bear, so the bear likes the mouse. Then, the tiger likes the mouse, but there's no direct link from the bear to liking the tiger. So, the bear's liking is only towards the mouse, not the tiger. Therefore, the statement is false because the bear does not like the tiger. So, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me re-examine.\n\nFrom the context:\n\n- The bear is big, needs the rabbit, sees the rabbit.\n\n- The rabbit is round, likes the tiger, needs the tiger.\n\n- The tiger is red, likes the mouse.\n\n- The mouse likes the tiger, needs the rabbit, sees the rabbit and the tiger.\n\nFrom implication 8: If X is round and sees bear \u2192 bear likes mouse. Rabbit is round and sees bear \u2192 bear likes mouse.\n\nSo, bear likes mouse.\n\nFrom implication 2: If X needs tiger \u2192 X likes mouse. Rabbit needs tiger \u2192 rabbit likes mouse.\n\nBut the rabbit also likes the tiger (fact 9). So, the rabbit likes both the mouse and the tiger.\n\nFrom implication 9: If X likes rabbit and rabbit needs tiger \u2192 tiger likes rabbit. Since the rabbit likes the tiger and needs the tiger, the tiger likes the rabbit.\n\nBut the bear's liking is only towards the mouse, not the tiger. So, the bear does not like the tiger. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe bear likes the mouse because the rabbit, who is round and sees the bear, causes the bear to like the mouse. The tiger likes the mouse, but there's no information indicating the bear likes the tiger. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nB) False",
